  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble members are in good agreement that by Tuesday drier air \nwill start to filter back into the region as the subtropical high \nstarts to retrograde back over our region. By the middle of the \nweek the center of the high will be straddling the AZ/NM border \nand by the end of the week the center of the high will be over AZ.\nAs the high moves overhead, PWATs will drop into a 0.8-1.2\" range\nacross our region and steering flow will be out of the S/SW. The \ncombination of these factors will lead to poor chances for \nthunderstorm development across the region. The NBM shows that \nTuesday and Wednesday there could be enough moisture around to \nsupport isolated thunderstorm chances for the higher terrain \nareas. But by the end of next week thunderstorm chances for our \narea shut off and will mainly be relegated to the Mogollon Rim. \n\nUnusually hot temperatures will be the main story next week. As \nmentioned above the subtropical high will slowly retrograde back \nover our region beginning on Tuesday, which will result in a \ngradual warming trend for our area. The high is also expected to \nstrengthen to around 596-598dam during this time. The combination \nof the center settling over our region and strengthening will \nresult in temperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) are \nforecasted to be in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the \nlower deserts and in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range across the \nhigher terrain areas. The increase in temperatures will also \nresult in an increase in HeatRisk, with widespread Extreme \nHeatRisk forecast for the majority of the CWA by Wednesday. There \nremains high confidence in the heat forecast for next week. So, \nthe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through next Friday and\nwill likely need to be expanded to more areas by the middle of \nthe week and may eventually need to be extended into next weekend.",
